content.content:
Groups switched off in the filter are not shown in any view.

### Moving an entry to another day

**In the month and the week, an entry is dragged onto another day.** A mouse picks it up by
moving it a few pixels; a finger by holding still on it for a moment, so a grid still scrolls
under a finger that only means to scroll — the same press-and-drag the board's cards use. The
day under it is marked, releasing moves the entry there, and the drop never also opens the entry
or the day preview. The day view has only one day to drop on and the year view draws coloured
days rather than entries, so neither has it. Only what is drawn can be picked up: an entry hidden
behind a month cell's `+3`, or listed in the day preview, is moved from its popup.

- **The day changes and the time does not.** A 09:00 meeting dropped on Thursday is at 09:00 on
  Thursday, read in the configured zone, so it is still 09:00 across a DST change. In the week the
  target is the day's column and only that: dropped lower or higher in the hour grid, the entry
  keeps its time.
- **A span moves whole.** Whichever of its days was picked up, **the day it is released on is its
  new start**, and its end moves by the same number of days: a three-day trip held by its middle
  day and dropped on a Friday runs Friday to Sunday.
- **A repeating entry moves this one occurrence**, as an override — the same thing the board does
  when it moves one. There is no "move the whole series" by dragging; that is the popup's.
- **A task's sprint follows its date**, as it always has: a task dragged out of its sprint's
  range is on the next board.

### The day preview

.rows { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; overflow: hidden; }
/* An entry is a **tint of its category's colour with that colour as the ink**, not a solid block
   of it: a month of solid bars is a month that cannot be read past, and the tint keeps the page
   paper-coloured while saying the same thing. The rule down the left is what carries the colour
   at full strength. `--c` is set on the element by views.js; the two mixes are tokens, because a
   dark page needs more tint and a lighter ink than a light one.
   The tint is mixed with `transparent` rather than with the background, so a line sits correctly
   on a weekend cell, which is not the same colour as the rest. */
.eline {
  display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .25rem;
  padding: .1rem .26rem .1rem .36rem; font-size: .68rem; line-height: 1.35;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c, var(--accent)) var(--chip-bg), transparent);
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c, var(--accent)) var(--chip-ink), var(--fg));
  box-shadow: inset 2px 0 0 var(--c, var(--accent));
  border-radius: .25rem; cursor: pointer;
}
.eline .when { flex: none; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; font-weight: 600; }
.eline .what { flex: 1; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; }
/* In a month cell the title is clipped rather than ellipsised: three dots cost three characters
   in the one place in the app where characters are scarcest, and a clipped word already says
   there is more. */
.grid .eline .what { text-overflow: clip; }
.eline.done .what { text-decoration: line-through; opacity: .75; }
/* A day an entry only passes through: flat on the side it continues, so a run of cells reads as
   one bar with two rounded ends. The rule down the left belongs to the day it starts on, so a
   continued day drops it and takes that space back for the title. */
.eline.from-before {
  border-top-left-radius: 0; border-bottom-left-radius: 0;
  box-shadow: none; padding-left: .26rem;
}
.eline.goes-on { border-top-right-radius: 0; border-bottom-right-radius: 0; }
.more { font-size: .6rem; color: var(--muted); padding-left: .2rem; }

/* The rectangular icon that moves a task straight to done. */
.tick {
  flex: none; width: .95rem; height: .72rem; margin: 0; padding: 0;
.tcol.we { background-color: var(--soft); }
.tev {
  position: absolute; left: 2px; right: 2px; min-height: 1rem; overflow: hidden;
  padding: .12rem .34rem; font-size: .68rem; line-height: 1.3;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c, var(--accent)) var(--chip-bg), var(--bg));
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c, var(--accent)) var(--chip-ink), var(--fg));
  box-shadow: inset 2.5px 0 0 var(--c, var(--accent));
  border-radius: .3rem; cursor: pointer;
}
.tev .when { display: block; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; font-weight: 600; }
.tev .what { display: block; font-weight: 600; }
.tev .meta { display: block; font-size: .62rem; opacity: .85; }
.tev.done .what { text-decoration: line-through; }
.tev .tick { position: absolute; top: .22rem; right: .22rem; }

/* ---- kanban ---- */
/* The board is capped and centred where the calendar window is as wide as the browser: a card
   is read at the width a sentence is read at, and a third of a wide monitor is mostly empty
   card. The cap is here rather than on #cal, which would narrow the pane with it. */
.kb {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: .5rem; padding: .5rem;
  max-width: 78rem; margin-inline: auto;
}
/* Stacked: the three stages one under the other, each full width, read by scrolling down. */
.kb.vert { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
.view.vK.one { overflow: hidden; }
/* Exactly one column is visible. The strip is shifted by whole multiples of its own width, so
   it must carry neither gap nor padding - either one leaves a sliver of the next column at the
   edge of the screen. The spacing lives inside each column instead. */
.one .kb {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 100%); gap: 0; padding: 0; max-width: none;
  transform: translateX(calc(var(--col) * -100%)); transition: transform .18s ease-out;
}
.one .kcol { margin: .5rem; }
/* No tray behind a column: the three stand on the page and are told apart by their headings and
   the rule under each, which is how a printed page separates three columns too. */
.kcol {
  display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .5rem;
  padding: .4rem; background: none; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: .7rem;
}
/* The state's name is centred on the column and carries that stage's colour; the `+` sits at the
   right, where it is the board's only way to make a task. Two equal side tracks, so the name is
   centred on the column rather than on what the `+` leaves over. */
.khead {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr; align-items: center; gap: .3rem;
  padding-bottom: .35rem; margin-bottom: .1rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
}
.kmid { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: .2rem; min-width: 0; }
.kname {
  font-family: var(--serif);
  text-transform: capitalize; font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.15;
  overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
/* The header's pagers are bigger and sit on a background of their own, so they cannot be read
   as a card's move buttons, which do something else entirely. `.ib` is in the base sheet now, so
   these win on order rather than losing to it - which is what makes the two sizes here the ones
   that apply. The background is set in the phone block below, where the pager fills the header;
   a disabled arrow keeps none, and that is what tells it from the one that still works. */
.knav { flex: none; width: 2.4rem; height: 2.4rem; }
.knav[disabled] { background: none; }
.knav svg { width: 1.5rem; height: 1.5rem; }
.kadd { justify-self: end; width: 2rem; height: 2rem; }
.kcards { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .4rem; }
.kcard {
  display: flex; gap: .45rem; padding: .5rem;
  background: var(--raise); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: .55rem;
  box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgb(90 60 30 / .06);
}
.kbar { flex: none; width: 3px; border-radius: 2px; }

/* ---- dragging ---- */
/* What can be held is not text to select and has no long-press menu, or a held finger would
   select a word or raise the system's callout instead of picking it up. */
.kcard, .eline, .tev, .topbar { -webkit-user-select: none; user-select: none; -webkit-touch-callout: none; }
.kcard, .eline, .tev { cursor: grab; }
/* The copy that follows the pointer, and the original it was lifted from. */
.drag-ghost {
  position: fixed; z-index: 20; margin: 0; pointer-events: none; opacity: .92;
  box-shadow: var(--shadow); transform: rotate(1deg);
}
.dragging { opacity: .35; }
/* The target under the pointer: a stage, a day, or one of the strip's three zones. */
.kcol.drop-on, .day.drop-on, .tcol.drop-on, .adcell.drop-on, .tgd.drop-on {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--link) 12%, transparent);
  outline: 2px dashed var(--link); outline-offset: -2px;
}

